---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import { withBase } from "astro-theme-university/url";
import Receipt from "../components/Receipt.astro";
import TermOverview from "../components/TermOverview.astro";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  mainAttrs={{ class: "home-main" }}
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <h2>What you will do</h2>
  <p>
    Every week names one specific public machine — a parking meter, an
    airport kiosk, an ATM, a fare gate — and asks two questions of it: what
    actually happens during the one transaction a stranger gets, and who
    benefits when that transaction goes badly. You'll do fieldwork on real
    machines, build an evidenced incentive map, and finish the semester by
    tearing down and redesigning one, with a memo naming who'd resist the
    change.
  </p>

  <h2>How this course runs</h2>
  <p>
    Each week pairs a lecture with a tutorial, both built around one machine.
    The lecture — delivered every week by Marisol Quaye — introduces that
    week's machine and names the incentive behind its worst behaviour. The
    tutorial, run every week by Idris Fenn, is where the fieldwork and
    teardown work actually happens: you go find the machine, use it, and
    bring back what you saw.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two assignments interrupt that rhythm to turn it into graded work — a
    field audit in week 4, an incentive case study in week 7 — and the
    course ends in week 12 with a full teardown and redesign of one machine
    from the semester. See the <a href={withBase("/sessions/")}>tutorials page</a> for
    the week-by-week schedule and the <a href={withBase("/assessments/")}>assessment page</a> for
    what each piece of work is worth.
  </p>
  <TermOverview />

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    Anyone who has stood at a ticket machine with a queue behind them and
    suspected the confusion wasn't an accident. No design background is
    assumed, but you should already be comfortable making an argument from
    evidence rather than instinct — this isn't an introduction to "good
    design," it's an audit of who a specific bad design is good for.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title="Lectures" href="/lectures/">
      <p>Every week's machine and the incentive argument made about it.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/sessions/">
      <p>The weekly schedule and what happens in each teaching tutorial.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>What is marked, what it is worth, and when it is due.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="People" href="/people/">
      <p>Who teaches the course and how to reach them.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>

  <Receipt>
    <p>
      This page is a one-shot interface too: three colours, a fixed layout,
      and a handful of decisions about what to put in front of you and what
      to leave in small print. If you can tell where that was done on
      purpose, you can probably tell it on the machines this course actually
      studies.
    </p>
  </Receipt>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  /* Homepage title as a physical readout: a parking-meter/ATM screen behind
     the h1, not a photo of one. `.home-main` comes from `mainAttrs` on
     ContentLayout so this stays scoped to the homepage's title only — the
     h1 itself lives outside this file's template (rendered by
     ContentLayout), so Astro's usual scoped-CSS hashing can't reach it. */
  :global(.home-main > h1) {
    /* The screen's dark panel lives on ::before, breaking out to the full
       viewport width — see there for why. The h1 box itself stays on
       .at-main's own span (inset-start/content-end) purely so its padding
       box is the coordinate system that ::before centres against, and so
       the text keeps the same left edge as the paragraphs below it
       (content-start, one content-inset to the right of the gold rule). */
    position: relative;
    isolation: isolate;
    grid-column: inset-start / content-end;
    margin-block: 0 var(--at-spacing-xl);
    padding-block: calc(var(--at-spacing-2xl) * 2);
    padding-inline: var(--at-content-inset) 0;
    font-family: var(--ledger-font);
    font-size: var(--at-font-size-h3);
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: #7cffb0;
    text-shadow:
      0 0 2px rgba(124, 255, 176, 0.9),
      0 0 10px rgba(124, 255, 176, 0.5),
      0 0 24px rgba(124, 255, 176, 0.25);
  }

  /* The dark screen panel: sized to the h1's own box height, but centred
     against .at-main's span (h1's containing block, itself centred between
     two equal flexible gutters) at full viewport width — the classic
     centred-container breakout, done on the background layer instead of
     the text so the words can stay aligned with the rest of the page. */
  :global(.home-main > h1)::before {
    content: "";
    position: absolute;
    z-index: -1;
    inset-block: 0;
    left: 50%;
    width: 100vw;
    transform: translateX(-50%);
    background:
      repeating-linear-gradient(
        to bottom,
        rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.03) 0px,
        rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.03) 1px,
        transparent 1px,
        transparent 3px
      ),
      #0a0f0c;
    border: 1px solid #1e2b23;
    border-radius: calc(var(--at-border-radius) / 2);
    box-shadow:
      inset 0 0 16px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.6),
      var(--at-shadow-sm);
  }

  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    font-family: var(--ledger-font);
    font-size: var(--at-font-size-xs);
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
  }
</style>
